outputs.exact_statement:
For the preceding choices, every nonzero homogeneous $h_I\in R_I$ of positive degree satisfies $$\label{cone:alpha}
 \mathop{\mathrm{lct}}_o(C_0;h_I)\geq\frac{1}{2n(I/a)}.$$ Every nonzero regular local function $g$ on $V$ and every divisorial or real quasi-monomial valuation $w$ centred at $o$ satisfy $$\label{cone:alpha-germ}
 w(g)\leq 2n A_{\theta_V}(w)\,v(g).$$ Moreover, for every valuation $w$ centred at $o$, $$\label{cone:comparison}
 w(g)\geq s(w)v(g)\quad(g\in\mathcal O_{V,o}),
 \qquad s(w)=\min_i\frac{w(h_i)}{v(h_i)}.$$ In particular each positive normalized homogeneous degree is at least $1/(2n)$.

After passage to a subsequence there is a uniform constant $C$ such that $$\label{prod:product-bound}
 h\,d_1\cdots d_n\leq C,
 \qquad h=\mathop{\mathrm{vol}}(v).$$

Suppose $q>0$ and $T=d_{m+1}$ begins the tier after the chosen complete prefix. Uniformly sufficiently far along that jump, $$\label{prod:adjoint-unique}
 h^0\bigl(F,K_F+\lceil bTD_F\rceil\bigr)=1
 \qquad(0<b\leq1/10,\ b\in\mathbb Q).$$

Put $d_{n+1}=+\infty$. There is a uniform constant $C_W$ such that $$\label{val:window-bound}
 \mathcal H(T):=\dim_{k_0}\mathcal O_{V,o}/(v>T)
 \le C_W\prod_{j\le m}\frac{T}{d_j}
 \quad(d_m\le T<d_{m+1},\ 1\le m\le n).$$

For regular eigenfunctions $t$ whose $w$-initial is algebraic over the graded tuple field of $f$, put $$\Lambda_f=\left\langle(\mathbf e_i,\chi_{f_i}):1\le i\le r
                     \right\rangle_{\mathbb Z}
 \subset\mathbb Q^r\times\operatorname{Hom}(G,k_0^*),$$ where $\mathbf e_i=m_{f_i}$ is the $i$th standard vector. Then $$\begin{aligned}
 \#\{(m_t,\chi_t)\bmod\Lambda_f\}&\le C_3,
 \label{round:U1}\\
 |m_{t,i}|&\le C_4\frac{w(t)}{d_i}\quad(1\le i\le r).
 \label{round:U2}
\end{aligned}$$

When $r=m$, the selected vertex satisfies $$\label{round:U3}
 \left|\frac{(e-\phi g_s)_i}{s}\right|
       \le C_6\frac d{d_i}\quad(1\le i\le m).$$ Consequently, on a selected ratio branch along a segment of weights, at its independent parameters, $$\label{round:derivative-bound}
 \left|\frac{d\Phi}{dt}\right|
 \le C_6\sum_{i\le m}\frac d{d_i}|\dot b_i|.$$
